ligence\" and pioneered the field of symbolic AI, which focused on using logic and rules to mimic human cognition. Papert, known for his work on artificial neural networks and the development of the Logo programming language, which introduced children to computational thinking, further expanded the scope of AI education and accessibility. These early contributions not only established MIT as a leader in AI research but also provided the theoretical underpinnings for subsequent advancements in the field.\n\nThe 1980s and 1990s marked another pivotal era for MIT\u2019s influence on AI, as the institute shifted its focus toward practical applications and the integration of AI into real-world systems. During this period, MIT researchers made significant strides in robotics, computer vision, and natural language processing (NLP). The institute\u2019s work in robotics, particularly through projects like the \"Mobile Robotics Group\" led by Rodney Brooks, revolutionized the field by emphasizing embodied intelligence\u2014the idea that intelligence is deeply tied to an agent\u2019s physical interaction with its environment. Brooks\u2019 subsumption architecture, which allowed robots to perform tasks without complex centralized planning, was a departure from traditional AI approaches and paved the way for more robust and adaptive robotic systems. This work culminated in the creation of robots like the \"Genghis\" and \"PackBot,\" which demonstrated remarkable autonomy and were later deployed in military and search-and-rescue operations. Meanwhile, MIT\u2019s contributions to computer vision, led by researchers like Berthold Horn and Shimon Ullman, advanced techniques for image recognition and understanding, laying the groundwork for modern applications in medical imaging, autonomous vehicles, and surveillance systems.\n\nIn the realm of natural language processing, MIT has been a key player in developing algorithms that enable machines to understand and generate human langua