r, revolutionized the consumer robotics market with products like the Roomba vacuum cleaner, which uses AI to navigate and clean homes autonomously. Another notable startup is Kiva Systems (now part of Amazon Robotics), which developed autonomous mobile robots for warehouse automation, significantly improving the efficiency of logistics operations. These successes underscore MIT\u2019s role in bridging the gap between academic research and commercial applications, ensuring that AI technologies are not only theoretically sound but also practical and scalable.\n\nIn addition to its contributions to technology and industry, MIT has been a leader in addressing the ethical, social, and policy implications of AI. As AI systems become more powerful and pervasive, questions about their impact on society\u2014ranging from job displacement and privacy concerns to bias and accountability\u2014have become increasingly urgent. MIT\u2019s scholars and researchers have been at the forefront of these discussions, advocating for responsible AI development and policy frameworks that prioritize human well-being. The MIT Initiative on the Digital Economy (IDE), launched in 2015, brings together economists, computer scientists, and social scientists to study the impact of digital technologies, including AI, on labor markets, inequality, and economic growth. The initiative\u2019s research has highlighted both the opportunities and challenges posed by AI, such as the potential for increased productivity and the need for policies that mitigate job displacement and ensure equitable access to AI-driven benefits.\n\nMIT\u2019s commitment to ethical AI is also reflected in its educational programs. The institute offers courses and degree programs that integrate technical AI training with discussions on ethics, fairness, and societal impact. For example, the \"Ethics of AI\" course, taught by philosophers and computer scientists, explores the moral implications of AI systems, including issues o